.Dd October 27, 2026
.Dt XTC_USDT 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_usdt
.Nd static probes for DTrace, perf, bpftrace and SystemTap
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libxtc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm ./dist/configure
.Op Fl -enable-dtrace Ns Op = Ns Ar yes | sdt | auto | no
.Op Ev DTRACE Ns = Ns Ar path
.Op Ev DTRACEFLAGS Ns = Ns Ar flags
.Pp
.Nm meson setup
.Fl Ddtrace Ns = Ns Ar no | auto | yes | sdt
.Pp
.Nm dtrace
.Fl n
.Sq xtc$target:::probe-name { ... }
.Fl c
.Ar program
.Pp
.Nm bpftrace
.Fl e
.Sq usdt:./program:xtc:probe__name { ... }
.Sh DESCRIPTION
libxtc can be built with a set of user-level statically defined tracing
(USDT) probes in its scheduler, mailbox, timer, I/O, lock and supervision
paths.
The probes are defined once, as the
.Sy xtc
provider in
.Pa src/xtc_probes.d ,
and serve two tracer families from one build:
.Bl -bullet -width 2n
.It
DTrace on FreeBSD, illumos (OmniOS, SmartOS) and macOS, which see them as
.Sy xtc Ns Em PID Ns Sy ::: Ns Em probe-name .
.It
perf, bpftrace, bcc and SystemTap on Linux, which read the
.Sy .note.stapsdt
ELF notes the same probes produce and see them as
.Sy xtc:probe__name
(double underscores; DTrace shows each
.Ql __
as a hyphen).
.El
.Pp
The probes are off by default.
In a build without them every probe macro expands to nothing and none of
its arguments is evaluated: the library's machine code is identical to a
build that has no probe sites at all.
.Ss Build modes
.Bl -tag -width "--enable-dtrace=auto"
.It Fl -enable-dtrace Ns = Ns Cm no
The default.
No probes.
.It Fl -enable-dtrace Ns = Ns Cm yes
Requires a
.Xr dtrace 1
program.
.Ql dtrace -h
generates the probe header.
Except on macOS,
.Ql dtrace -G
then rewrites each probe call site in the library objects to no-ops and
emits the probe table into
.Pa xtc_probes.o ,
which is archived into
.Pa libxtc.a
and linked into the shared library.
Every object with a probe site references an anchor symbol in
.Pa xtc_probes.o ,
so a program linked statically against
.Pa libxtc.a
always carries the probe table.
On Linux this mode works with the systemtap
.Nm dtrace
script, which also emits the is-enabled semaphores.
.It Fl -enable-dtrace Ns = Ns Cm sdt
Linux, header-only: the systemtap
.In sys/sdt.h
(package systemtap-sdt-dev or systemtap-sdt-devel) emits the notes
directly; no tool runs at build time.
There are no semaphores, so
.Fn XTC_PROBE_ENABLED
is always true and probe arguments are computed at every site; they are
cheap by design (see
.Sx Cost ) .
.It Fl -enable-dtrace Ns = Ns Cm auto
.Cm yes
if a working
.Nm dtrace
is found, else
.Cm sdt
if a systemtap
.In sys/sdt.h
is, else
.Cm no .
.El
.Pp
Tested: FreeBSD with
.Cm yes ,
Linux with
.Cm sdt .
Linux with
.Cm yes
(the systemtap
.Nm dtrace
script), macOS and illumos follow the same recipe but were not run when the
probes were added.
.Pp
.Ev DTRACE
names the program and
.Ev DTRACEFLAGS
passes flags to it (for example
.Ql DTRACEFLAGS=-64
on illumos), as in PostgreSQL's configure.
On Windows the option is refused; ETW is the analogue there and is not
provided.
.Pp
The single-file amalgamation
.Pq Pa xtc.c
has no configure step.
Compile it with
.Fl DXTC_PROBES_SDT
on Linux to get the notes.
For DTrace, run
.Ql dtrace -h -s src/xtc_probes.d
into
.Pa xtc_probes_gen.h ,
rename its
.Dv XTC_
macros to
.Dv XTC_DT_
the way
.Pa dist/xtc_probes.sh
does, save it as
.Pa xtc_probes_dtrace.h ,
compile with
.Fl DXTC_HAVE_DTRACE ,
and on FreeBSD or illumos run
.Ql dtrace -G -s src/xtc_probes.d -o probes.o xtc.o
and link
.Pa probes.o
next to
.Pa xtc.o .
.Ss Argument conventions
.Bl -tag -width "object"
.It Fa pid , gen
A libxtc pid in two 64-bit arguments:
.Fa pid
is
.Ql loop_id << 32 | local_id
and the next argument is its 64-bit generation
.Pq Fa gen , from_gen , to_gen , parent_gen , child_gen .
The pair is the identity: the slot of an exited process is reused, with
a higher generation.
Both 0 is
.Dv XTC_PID_NONE .
Through libxtc 1.x the pid was one argument,
.Ql loop_id << 48 | local_id << 32 | gen ;
2.0 widened the pid to 16 bytes.
.It Fa task
The fiber's
.Vt xtc_task_t *
as an integer.
It is stable for the fiber's life and is the key that joins
.Sy fiber-park ,
.Sy fiber-wake ,
.Sy fiber-resume
and the run probes.
Key D variables on it rather than on the thread: a migratable fiber can
park on one OS thread and resume on another.
.It Fa loop
The executor loop index, or \-1 for a loop that is not part of an
.Xr xtc_exec 3
executor.
.El
.Pp
No probe argument reads a clock.
Take time from the tracer
.Po D
.Va timestamp ,
bpftrace
.Va nsecs
.Pc .
That keeps the probes out of the deterministic simulator's determinism
guard, and under the simulator the
.Fa deadline_ns
and
.Fa now_ns
arguments of the timer probes are virtual time.
.Ss Probes
.Bl -tag -width 2n
.It Sy proc-spawn Ns Pq Fa pid , gen , parent , parent_gen
A process was created
.Pq Xr xtc_proc_spawn 3 .
.Fa parent
is the spawning process, 0 if it was spawned from outside one.
.It Sy proc-start Ns Pq Fa pid , gen , task
The process began running on its fiber.
.It Sy proc-exit Ns Pq Fa pid , gen , reason
The process finished with exit
.Fa reason ,
before links and monitors are notified.
.It Sy proc-kill Ns Pq Fa target , gen , reason
.Xr xtc_exit_pid 3
was called on a live process.
.It Sy msg-send Ns Pq Fa from , from_gen , to , to_gen , bytes
A message was queued for delivery
.Pq Xr xtc_send 3 .
.It Sy msg-recv Ns Pq Fa pid , gen , from , from_gen , bytes
A process took a message from its mailbox.
.It Sy fiber-park Ns Pq Fa task , reason , object
A fiber is about to yield because it must wait.
.Fa reason
is one of 1 mailbox
.Pq Xr xtc_recv 3 ,
2 sleep
.Pq Xr xtc_proc_sleep 3 ,
3 fd
.Pq Xr xtc_proc_wait_fd 3 ,
.Fa object
is the fd), 4 aio
.Pq Xr xtc_aio 3 ;
.Fa object
is the request, matching
.Sy aio-submit ) ,
5 blocking pool
.Pq Fa object No is the work item ,
matching
.Sy blocking-start ) ,
6 sync primitive (amutex, arwlock, semaphore, notify, barrier, gate;
.Fa object
is the primitive's internal lock), 7 lwlock
.Pq Fa object No is the lock ,
8 lock manager
.Pq Fa object No is the manager ,
9 future
.Pq Fa object No is the future .
A fiber may park and resume several times for one wait: it rechecks its
condition after every resume.
.It Sy fiber-resume Ns Pq Fa task , reason , object
The fiber is running again after the matching
.Sy fiber-park .
The time between the two is time spent parked plus time spent runnable
but not yet scheduled.
.It Sy fiber-wake Ns Pq Fa task , loop , how
A waker targeted the fiber.
.Fa how
is 0 when the waker made it runnable on this thread's loop, 1 when it
posted it to another loop's inbox, and 2 when the fiber was not parked
(the wake was latched for a park still in progress, or the fiber was
already runnable).
A
.Sy fiber-park
followed by no
.Sy fiber-wake
means the wake source never fired; one followed by a wake with
.Fa how
0 or 1 and no
.Sy fiber-resume
means the wake was lost after it was generated.
.It Sy task-run-start Ns Pq Fa task , loop , lane
The scheduler is about to run a task quantum.
.Fa lane
is its scheduling class, \-1 for the default lane.
.It Sy task-run-done Ns Pq Fa task , loop , verdict
The quantum returned: 0 done, 1 rescheduled (yield), 2 parked.
.It Sy loop-poll Ns Pq Fa loop , timeout_ns , nevents
A blocking poll of the loop's I/O backend returned
.Fa nevents
events; \-1
.Fa timeout_ns
waits forever.
Non-blocking fairness polls are not reported.
.It Sy io-dispatch Ns Pq Fa loop , fd , flags , task
An I/O event was dispatched to the task waiting for it
.Pf ( Fa fd
is \-1 for an AIO completion).
.It Sy sched-steal Ns Pq Fa thief , victim , task
A loop took a migratable task from another loop's queue.
.It Sy timer-arm Ns Pq Fa timer , deadline_ns , task
A timer was armed:
.Fa task
is the fiber a park timer will wake, 0 for an
.Xr xtc_timer_set 3
callback timer.
.It Sy timer-fire Ns Pq Fa timer , deadline_ns , now_ns
A timer fired;
.Fa now_ns No \- Fa deadline_ns
is its lateness on the libxtc monotonic clock.
.It Sy timer-cancel Ns Pq Fa timer
A pending timer was cancelled before it fired.
.It Sy aio-submit Ns Pq Fa aio , op , fd , len
A file operation reached the native AIO backend.
.Fa op :
0 pread, 1 pwrite, 2 fsync, 3 fdatasync, 4 preadv, 5 pwritev.
.Fa len
is 0 for the vector and sync operations.
An operation the backend refused falls back to the blocking pool and
fires the blocking probes instead.
.It Sy aio-done Ns Pq Fa aio , op , res
The submitting fiber saw the result: bytes, or a negative errno.
.It Sy blocking-enqueue Ns Pq Fa work , qlen
Work was queued for the blocking pool
.Pq Xr xtc_blocking_run 3 ;
.Fa qlen
is the queue length after it.
.It Sy blocking-start Ns Pq Fa work
A pool thread began running it.
.It Sy blocking-done Ns Pq Fa work , result
The pool thread finished it.
.It Sy lock-wait-start Ns Pq Fa lock , kind , mode , waiter
A caller blocked on a contended lock: it is about to sleep on a condition
variable or park its fiber.
.Fa kind :
1
.Xr xtc_amutex 3 ,
2 arwlock, 3
.Xr xtc_lwlock 3 ,
4
.Xr xtc_lockmgr 3
(then
.Fa lock
is the locked object, not the manager).
.Fa mode :
0 for an amutex; 0 shared, 1 exclusive for an arwlock; the
.Vt xtc_lwlock_mode_t
or
.Vt xtc_lock_mode_t
otherwise.
.Fa waiter
is the parking fiber's task, 0 for a waiting OS thread.
Uncontended acquisitions do not fire; trace those with the pid provider
or a uprobe on the function.
.It Sy lock-wait-done Ns Pq Fa lock , kind , mode , waiter
The caller woke up: it holds the lock, will recheck and may wait again
(one start/done pair per sleep), or timed out.
.It Sy sup-restart Ns Pq Fa sup , child , child_gen , reason
A supervisor is restarting after
.Fa child
exited with
.Fa reason
.Pq Xr xtc_sup 3 .
.It Sy preempt-tick Ns Pq Fa involuntary
A preemption timer tick arrived
.Pq Xr xtc_preempt 3 :
1 if it preempted the running fiber in signal context, 0 if it was
deferred to the next cooperative yield check.
Fires in a signal handler.
.It Sy alloc-fail Ns Pq Fa bytes
A libxtc heap allocation failed.
.It Sy slab-grow Ns Pq Fa slab , bytes
A slab cache mapped a new chunk
.Pq Xr xtc_slab 3 .
.It Sy sim-nondeterminism Ns Pq Fa what
Under the deterministic simulator, a sim-reachable path called a
nondeterministic primitive
.Fa ( what
is a string naming it).
The run aborts right after unless strict mode is off.
.It Sy sim-buggify Ns Pq Fa site , fired
Under the simulator, a buggify site was reached for the first time in the
run and its seeded decision made
.Fa ( site
is a string).
.El
.Ss Stability
The provider name, probe names, argument order and the numeric codes
above are an interface: they change only in a minor release, with a note
in the release notes, and an existing probe's arguments are never
reordered.
New probes and new codes may be added.
The module and function components of a probe (the
.Xr dtrace 1
.Ar mod
and
.Ar func
fields) are private: they name libxtc's internal functions and can change
in any release.
The D stability attributes in
.Pa src/xtc_probes.d
say the same: Evolving for the provider, names and arguments, Private for
module and function.
.Ss Cost
Disabled, a DTrace probe is a few no-op instructions where the call used
to be, plus the argument setup the compiler emitted for it; an SDT probe
is one
.Sy nop .
Probes sit on the slow paths: park, wake, a contended lock, a timer
firing, a failed allocation, a supervisor restart.
Fast paths (an uncontended lock, a send to a running process, a slab
hit) carry none.
Arguments are registers and plain loads, never a call or a clock read;
wrap anything more expensive in
.Fn XTC_PROBE_ENABLED NAME .
A build without probes contains none of this.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "tools/bpftrace/*.bt" -compact
.It Pa src/xtc_probes.d
The probe definitions.
.It Pa src/inc/xtc_probes.h
The macros at each probe site (library-internal).
.It Pa dist/xtc_probes.sh
The
.Ql dtrace -h
and
.Ql dtrace -G
steps.
.It Pa tools/dtrace/*.d
D scripts: park latency by reason, lock contention by lock with stacks,
timer lateness, AIO latency per operation, stranded fibers.
.It Pa tools/bpftrace/*.bt
The same for bpftrace on Linux.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Count messages and park reasons in a running program:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
dtrace -qn 'xtc$target:::msg-send { @["sent"] = count(); }
    xtc$target:::fiber-park { @why[arg1] = count(); }' -p 1234
.Ed
.Pp
Park-to-resume latency by reason:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
dtrace -qs tools/dtrace/xtc_park_latency.d -c ./server
.Ed
.Pp
The same on Linux with bpftrace, the binary linked against
.Pa libxtc.a
built with
.Fl -enable-dtrace=sdt :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bpftrace -e 'usdt:./server:xtc:fiber__park { @[arg1] = count(); }'
.Ed
.Pp
List the probes perf sees and record one.
.Ql perf probe
names the event with each double underscore collapsed to one, so record
.Ql sdt_xtc:msg_send ,
not
.Ql sdt_xtc:msg__send :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
perf buildid-cache --add ./server
perf list 'sdt_xtc:*'
perf probe -x ./server sdt_xtc:msg__send
perf record -e sdt_xtc:msg_send -g ./server
.Ed
.Pp
perf 7.2 cannot enable a probe that has more than one site in the
binary (for example
.Ql lock__wait__start ,
which fires from four lock paths): the second site gets the same
collapsed event name and the kernel refuses it.
Use bpftrace or SystemTap for those probes.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dtrace 1 ,
.Xr xtc_stats 3 ,
.Xr xtc_tail 3 ,
.Xr xtc_trace 3 ,
.Xr xtc 7
.Sh HISTORY
The probes first appeared in libxtc 1.59.0.
.Sh CAVEATS
On FreeBSD and illumos,
.Ql dtrace -l
and
.Ql dtrace -n
on a user process need root, or the dtrace_user privilege on illumos.
On macOS, System Integrity Protection restricts DTrace on system
binaries; your own programs are traceable once DTrace is allowed.
On Linux, perf and bpftrace need
.Dv CAP_PERFMON
or root.
A program that loads libxtc as a shared library registers its probes at
load time; one linked statically carries them in its own image.
